Choupo was conceived and architected by Vítor Geraldes.

Substantial parts of the initial implementation, guides, and tutorial corpus
were produced with assistance from Anthropic Claude Code using Claude
Opus/Fable models.

This is an AI-assisted work, not an autonomous AI work. The scientific and
engineering choices, architecture, prompts, acceptance/rejection, corrections,
licensing, trademark strategy, and release responsibility are human decisions.
The acknowledgement is deliberately explicit because pretending either "a
human typed every line" or "an AI authored the project" would be false.

This is the human-readable authorship record for the Choupo software and its
manuals. The authoritative fine-grained implementation record is the git
history.

Project lead and architect
  Vítor Geraldes

Software, cases, tests, models, and project maintenance
  Vítor Geraldes

Properties Guide and Theory Guide
  Vítor Geraldes
  Pedro Mendes
  Miguel Rodrigues

EduTools Guide
  Vítor Geraldes

Curators
  A curator reviews one named part of a guide or tool for correctness and
  answers for that review. Curation in this sense is not authorship: a curator
  holds no copyright in the work, makes no claim to it, and is deliberately
  absent from the author lists above and from every guide's copyright line. A
  name appears here only after the person has consented; the consent itself is
  a fact outside this repository and no check can verify it.

  Mónica Faria, Departamento de Engenharia Química, Instituto Superior Técnico
    EduTools Guide — the batch UF/NF membrane tool. Consent given to the
    project lead and recorded here 2026-09-24.

AI-assisted implementation
  Anthropic Claude Code / Claude Opus and Fable models
  Used as software-generation tools; not treated as human authors or
  copyright holders.

Contribution policy
  The upstream project is maintainer-led and does not accept external code or
  pull requests. Bug reports and improvement suggestions are welcome but do
  not create software or guide authorship. Forks may be developed and
  redistributed under GPL-3.0-or-later with their own authorship records.
